_implementation":"contradicts_reality","dod_uncertifiable":"certification_cannot_prove","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"high","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Add a host-independent proving check that the Windows collection guard exists and is scoped to fcntl only (e.g. assert the module skips under a simulated missing-fcntl / sys.platform=='win32' condition, or a static grep/AST assertion), so correctness is provable without waiting for the Windows sweep.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5b","T5e"],"criteria":["E6"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.2,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["'use a manually dispatched or Gerrit CI Windows sweep if available and rely on CI evidence for the final Windows proof'","AC4: 'Local targeted validation passes for the changed test module and relevant lock-kernel tests on this host' — on a POSIX host the guard branch never executes","The pytest command exercises fcntl-present behavior, not the Windows fcntl-absent skip"],"finding":"The fix targets Windows collection behavior, but all local validation runs on a POSIX host where the fcntl import succeeds and the new guard/skip is a no-op — so local tests cannot exercise the changed Windows path. The plan defers to Gerrit/Windows CI but does not specify a host-independent static check that the guard is actually present and correctly scoped.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f1e65b17e6ae50835","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Testing / Verification; AC4","norm_id":"n07ef129877e3d050","priority":0.75,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Guard is written but mis-scoped (e.g. skips whole module on all platforms, or guards the wrong import) and POSIX tests still pass, masking the defect until the next scheduled Windows sweep"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Add a POSIX-runnable test that monkeypatches/simulates the Windows condition (or importlib-blocks fcntl) and asserts the module is skipped rather than erroring, and record it as a Testing/Verification command.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.75,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"missing_oracle","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"uncertifiable_outcome","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"medium","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Specify that the fix skips the ENTIRE module on non-POSIX (e.g. a module-level `pytest.mark.skipif(sys.platform=='win32', ...)`), not merely guarding the `import fcntl`, because the test body calls `fcntl.flock` at runtime (lines 57-59) and would raise AttributeError on Windows if only the import were guarded.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.1831,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["test file line 4 `import fcntl` (collection break) plus lines 55-59 `os.open(...)` + `fcntl.flock(fd, ...)` (runtime use)","Plan step 2: 'Add a module-level Windows collection guard for this POSIX-only test module, or otherwise move the fcntl import behind a POSIX-only skip'","A try/except-guarded import (fcntl=None) fixes collection but not the runtime fcntl.flock calls, so the test still errors when executed on Windows"],"finding":"The plan presents two mechanisms as interchangeable ('a module-level Windows collection guard ... or otherwise move the fcntl import behind a POSIX-only skip'), but a bare guarded import alone is insufficient: the test body invokes fcntl.flock at runtime (lines 57-59), so with fcntl=None on Windows collection would succeed only to fail with AttributeError when the test runs. Because the test's purpose is a POSIX-only fcntl regression, the complete fix is a module-level platform skip that prevents the whole module from RUNNING on Windows, not just importing.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f8d9e312444b7e6d4","impact":0.536,"is_primary":null,"location":"Plan step 2 / AC line 2 ('The offending test module or fixture is portable at collection time on Windows; if skipped...')","norm_id":"n4e00eeedf713d389","priority":0.4169,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Windows sweep collects the module after a bare guarded import, then errors at runtime on fcntl.flock instead of skipping"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Scope the fix to a module-level `pytestmark = pytest.mark.skipif(sys.platform == 'win32', reason='POSIX-only fcntl leg regression; cross-platform lock path covered by tests/unit/store/test_windows_exclusive_leg.py')` combined with a guarded `try: import fcntl / except ImportError: fcntl = None`, matching the sibling idiom in tests/unit/test_snapshot_janitor.py.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.7778,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alter